  "inputs": {
    "manifest": {
      "specVersion": "1",
      "id": "lax-303502",
      "leanVersion": "v4.33.0",
      "mathlibVersion": "db584cd6d46c92f209a44c0f1c829460d327499d",
      "title": "Direct proofs for planar graph classes",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Clemens Kuske"
        }
      ],
      "bibEntries": [
        "@book{Diestel2025,\n  author = {Reinhard Diestel},\n  title = {Graph Theory},\n  edition = {6},\n  publisher = {Springer},\n  year = {2025},\n  url = {https://diestel-graph-theory.com/}\n}\n",
        "@techreport{PachTorocsik1992,\n  author = {J{\\'a}nos Pach and Jen{\\H{o}} T{\\\"o}r{\\H{o}}csik},\n  title = {Layout of Rooted Trees},\n  institution = {Princeton University},\n  number = {CS-TR-369-92},\n  year = {1992},\n  url = {https://www.cs.princeton.edu/techreports/1992/369.pdf}\n}\n"
      ]
    },
    "abstract": "This submission gives direct geometric proofs that every finite tree is\nouterplanar and that every finite star is outerplanar and planar. All drawings\nplace vertices on the unit circle using a rational parametrization.\n\nFor trees, induction removes a leaf and then inserts it beside its neighbour\nin a gap between the existing circle parameters. An affine functional for\nthe new chord separates it from all edges with disjoint endpoints. A tangent\nfunctional rules out vertices inside edges. The star proof is also given\nseparately: all its edges share the centre.\n\nThe proofs discharge the original statements in [Planar Graph Classes\n(Lax68)](https://laxarchive.org/lax-68/index.html), without changing its\ndefinitions or assuming any open characterization theorem. Each proof is\nadded to the archive after kernel validation.\n"
  },
  "requiredByConcepts": [],
  "requiredByProofs": [
    "Lax68"
  ],
  "concepts": [],
  "proofs": [
    {
      "id": "Lax303502Proofs.star_outerplanar",
      "path": "proofs/Lax303502Proofs/Stars.lean",
      "conclusion": "Lax68.StarOuterplanar.star_outerplanar",
      "assumptions": [],
      "description": "Every finite star has a straight-line drawing with its vertices on a circle.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Proof strategy",
          "markdown": "Choose distinct real parameters for the vertices and use the rational\nparametrization of the unit circle. A tangent to the circle at any vertex\nstrictly separates that vertex from every chord with different endpoints.\nAll edges of the star contain its centre, so there are no vertex-disjoint\nedges whose segments could cross. The construction includes the one-vertex\nstar."
        },
        {
          "title": "Attribution",
          "markdown": "Direct elementary circle construction. Diestel, *Graph Theory*, sixth edition,\nChapter 4, supplies the plane-drawing framework and the outerplanarity\ndefinition in Exercise 23, but does not give this explicit parametrization.\nThe coordinate proof here is supplied in full and uses no planarity theorem."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "Lax303502Proofs.star_planar",
      "path": "proofs/Lax303502Proofs/Stars.lean",
      "conclusion": "Lax68.StarPlanar.star_planar",
      "assumptions": [],
      "description": "Every finite star is planar.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Proof strategy",
          "markdown": "Use the explicit circle drawing from `star_outerplanar` and retain its\nstraight-line drawing certificate."
        },
        {
          "title": "Attribution",
          "markdown": "Immediate consequence of the circle construction in this submission."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "Lax303502Proofs.tree_outerplanar",
      "path": "proofs/Lax303502Proofs/Trees.lean",
      "conclusion": "Lax68.TreeOuterplanar.tree_outerplanar",
      "assumptions": [],
      "description": "Every finite tree admits a straight-line drawing with all vertices on the unit\ncircle, independently of the excluded-minor characterization.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Proof strategy",
          "markdown": "Induct on the number of vertices. A nontrivial finite tree has a leaf, and\nremoving it leaves a tree. Keep the smaller tree's circle drawing and insert\nthe leaf immediately after its neighbour in the parameter order. Finiteness\nprovides a gap with no other vertex. The chord for the new edge strictly\nseparates all other old vertices from the empty circular cap, so it misses\nevery edge with disjoint endpoints. The same-circle tangent inequality rules\nout vertices lying on edges. Single-vertex trees are handled directly."
        },
        {
          "title": "Attribution",
          "markdown": "Diestel, *Graph Theory*, sixth edition, Section 1.5, gives the leaf-removal\ninduction. Chapter 4, Exercise 23, states the outerplanarity characterization\nbut does not supply a direct drawing proof. As an additional source for the\ngeometric construction, Pach and Törőcsik, *Layout of rooted trees*,\nCS-TR-369-92 (1992), page 2, Algorithm 1, recursively embed a tree into points\nin convex position. Here the specialization to a circle is implemented by\nleaf insertion and explicit rational coordinates; the separation algebra is\nproved in this submission."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
